2200 celiac assays with a modest  sample size they demonstrated good  sensitivity  using a known celiac population and good  specificity using residual samples from  auto antibody screening as their control  population  this study also highlighted an important  feature that i forgot to mention about  the biplex assay which is its  iga deficiency detection bead so when  you measure ttg iga  using the beads there's a control bead  in there that  measures iga  as well and it will flag not give you a  result if iga  is not detected  and then this allows your lab to  set up a protocol for for when that  happens in our lab  we have a reflex  test that gets ordered  automatically when this flag is detected  that adds on an iga and reports a  comment um that ttg is unreliable uh  without  iga  the question remains can we  avoid biopsy in pediatric patients can  we apply a serological diagnosis  using  an upper limit of normal cut off  well the answer is it's complicated  there are few north american studies  that looked at this  the top plot here  shows  a multi-site study it's relatively small  that included 240 patients  over five different  sites health centers methodology was not  collected as part of the study so it  definitely demonstrates  real  life variation and practices  and what they found was even with it  upper limit  10 times upper limit of normal  cut off  the positive predictive value  was  not as good as  the europeans were describing in the  initial studies so 87.7  [Music]  and the specificity was quite low when  you would expect  um a higher specificity  when you're cut off is 10 times higher  another study out of calgary  this bottom table here  used used one assay had they had  definitely a bigger sample size a  thousand patients total  and they were using the euro immune  elisa  assay and they looked at different cut  off levels  overall and then also  excluding nodes that did not have a  positive ema and what they found was  that this  with this assay um there was relatively  good positive predictive value 95.9  percent and that positive predictive  value increased only modestly  [Applause]  if you required a positive ema  and the message  remains that we need local validation  for this 10 times the upper limit of  normal cutoff to account for population  differences as well as  variation and methodologies there's this  nike nice quote  from a paper that came out a few years  ago  regarding this  this cut off  saying that this inconsistency  in assays could lead to clinical  uncertainty unless individual  laboratories locally validate the tests  they decide to use this perspective is  supported by studies that have  demonstrated a wide variability of the  upper limit of normals when comparing  different commercially available  anti-ttg  assays  and so that is what we wanted to do  we put together a study in collaboration  with our  celiac clinic  that  looked at  retrospective  samples so we we pulled all ttg iga  samples from an 18 month time period  that included patients who had ttg iga  ordered and who also  received  a duodenal biopsy within six months of  that initial  ttg  so in this time period we have we  performed a total of 64 6 400  tgg igas on unique patients  567 of those patients also had a  duodenal biopsy within six months  and we ended up excluding 26 patients  for the following reasons  uh 16 because this was not their first  ttg iga they had an original  test performed at an outside lab  seven of these patients were iga  deficient and two had did not have  histology slides  available  for our study pathologist to review  that left us with 542  patients to analyze  84 of these patients were diagnosed with  celiac disease and 464 patients  had alternate diagnoses  as part of the study  my art my co-author and the study  pathologist dr pacheco  reviewed each pathology  slides again and independently  staged them  on the march staging scale  so i just want to take a second to talk  about  the what the biopsy process is like  so this this picture demonstrates um the  small intestine here that you the  duodenum uh is right after  um the stomach here it's this u-shaped  it's this u-shape  portion of the small intestine and then  the small intestine goes on here now  what's important is that within the  small intestine you normally have these  healthy finger like  called villi  and that is how  nutrients get absorbed  if you have celiac disease untreated  celiac disease these fingers these villi  are blunted  and and therefore they you lose quite a  bit of surface area and nutrients  um no longer can absorb  and that's why malabsorption is one of  the uh  one of the symptoms we try to  avoid  especially in pediatrics when treating  celiac disease  so  the gastroenterology will do an  endoscopy  and they'll insert a camera and a tube  down  with some forceps and what they're  supposed to do is take  five a minimum of five biopsy samples  it'll pinch off little bits of tissue  along the duodenum so this upper part of  the small intestine and at least one one  of those five should be from the bulb  and the bulb is what connects  to the stomach  and this is important taking enough  samples because celiac disease is known  to be with what is called apache disease  meaning that you may not you may miss it  if you're not looking at various parts  along along a duodenum  these slides  represent what marsh  scoring looks like  for the different stages and i just want  to thank christina pacheco our  pathologist  my colleague who provided these images  so you can see how in marsh zero you can  see the normal  villi how they're separated  the kind of bright pink areas those are  intra-epithelial lymphocytes and this is  marsh zero has  normal  march 1 you can start to see an increase  in intra-epithelial lymphocytes and then  all the way to marsh  3  you can see  atrophy or blunting of the villi  structures  as well as elongated  cellularity within the lamina propria  in our study which i'll show you the  results in a minute we didn't have any  patients who had march 3 which is good  because that is quite advanced  so these first results i want to share  are using the manufacturer cutoff so 15  units per milliliter  looking at sensitivity and specificity  for ttg iga  and so what you can see  uh  is the  marsh score  greater than 2 is considered positive  for celiac disease  and 15 or greater is considered  considered positive for the ttg iga test  and sensitivity  and specificity were both greater than  92 percent  the other numbers in these brackets here  i should point out these are biopsy  fragments that had  five or more taken  not every  biopsy  comes with the recommended number  of samples  and so we calculated sensitivity and  specificity for the total as well as for  those that had  the preferred amount of samples that  were taken  the negative predictive value  was great just so we would expect 97  and the positive predictive value is  about 72 percent  now using the 10 times upper limit of  normal as the cutoff  we found very high specificity for  celiac disease of 99.9  um  with a very good positive predictive  value of about 98  uh  basically only two  so you can see from the table i'll just  highlight for you  we had two positive  ten times upper limit of normal patients  that did not have a marsh score of two  or greater  so some considerations that i want to  highlight  for this study is one we did not include  ema or hla typing  in our calculations for sensitivity and  specificity and this is really due to  our institutional usage of these tests  we don't routinely test ema  at all it's not even a defined  test and the requests right now are  really are really limited to those um  to those patients where they are  considering a serologic diagnosis for  one reason or another  um the prevalence of celiac disease and  this relatively enhanced population was  15  and that and i want to point out again  that's why i think uh  the sensitivity and specificity of 92  percent that's really quite impressive  because this is a real  population of  patients who were being screened for  some re some reason enough that they  went on to  get a biopsy  so we would expect  to lose a little specificity you know  it's not a completely normal  population  so i think that's great and then with  the 10 times upper limit the specificity  is is quite high um the prevalence of  iga deficiency in our population we had  those seven that were excluded from the  study  was 1.3 percent  so the main takeaway here  is the biopsies that could be avoided  when implementing  a serologic diagnosis um  using a 10 times upper limit of normal  cut off so we had 44 patients that met  this criteria 42 of which  um  knowing you know having hindsight  in our hands we would know that we could  have made the diagnosis without a biopsy  two of these were technically  false positives both of them had marsh  one scoring so they weren't  marsh zeros they definitely had some  abnormal findings  and one of these false positives had was  a patient  with type 1 diabetes who was diagnosed  in the bath in the biopsy to have h  pylori gastritis  and this is  similar to other studies that have found  that h pylori gastritis can also cause  false positives the other false positive  i put in quotes had a marsh one lesion  but the patient was diagnosed with  celiac disease  due to their other symptoms and  resolution on a gluten-free  diet so  in our clinical practice  only one of these would have been  falsely diagnosed  and then i just want to also highlight  from this study and other studies from  our group  that patients with type 1 diabetes  should be considered separate from this  group undergoing consideration for  asymptomatic serologic diagnosis  because we just don't have enough  information on this group and we do know  they tend to have moderately elevated  ttg  but don't yet have symptoms of celiac  disease  and with tha