l  yes  so that's a great question actually for  auto antibody assays  it can be quite challenging to perform  quality assurance over time  so we use several strategies so we have  of course our  negative and positive control that was  done as part of every run  these are usually manufacturer based  controls and the limitation with those  is that they can be non-commutable so  usually they're purified samples they're  not  like real serum samples  so  some labs also choose to use external  controls this could be either serum  pools or it could be  purchased kind of a  serum based or serum matrix based  controls  additionally  we perform in the lab and  we're required through  various regulation bodies  when we get a new batch of reagent which  is considered a new lot of reagent to do  a lot to lot validation  and that helps ensure consistency as we  switch from one lot to another  and for this every lab practice can be  actually different in our lab we'll take  10 samples  across the range of the assay and we'll  compare them  to make sure that there's consistency um  and the criteria for acceptability could  be different depending on how  quantitatively consistent your assay is  anywhere from like 15 to 30 percent may  be considered acceptable in terms of the  difference um and another challenge with  other antibody assays is that we are  sometimes talking about  semi-quantitative assays so that can be  different from other immunoassays where  we'll see a precision of  five percent uh or extremely consistent  performance over a very long period of  time  and that's something that's important to  keep in mind that these assays are  semi-quantitative so we may see  more a bit more variability over time  and a higher imprecision than that  so hopefully that answers the question  perfect thank you so much  um another question for dr dickerson um  would her gis and her lab bring up ema  in-house in order to move to serological  diagnosis that ema is a component that  is